--> Can a JavaScript object hold a function as a property? Explain with an example.

 Yes. A JavaScript object can absolutely hold a function as a property. 
 When a function is stored as an object property, it is commonly called a method.

 let person = {
    name: "Shaliza",
    age: 25,
    greet: function() {
        console.log("Hello!");
    }
};

person.greet();

---> What are anonymous functions in JavaScript? Define their syntax and implementation.

An anonymous function is a function without a name.
It is usually assigned to a variable, passed as an argument to another function, or used as a callback.

let greet = function() {
    console.log("Hello");
};

greet();

What is an object?
A JavaScript object stores data as key-value pairs and can also contain functions/methods.
let user = {
    name: "John",
    age: 30,
    login: function() {
        console.log("Login successful");
    }
};

What is this?
Here this refers to the object that is calling the method.
let user = {
    name: "John",

    greet() {
        console.log(this.name);
    }
};
user.greet();

What is inheritance?
Child inherits the method from Parent.

What are try, catch, and finally?
try → code that might throw an error
catch → handles the error
finally → executes whether an error occurs or not
try {
    let result = riskyOperation();
} catch (error) {
    console.log(error);
} finally {
    console.log("Execution completed");
}